\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The edge is entrywise positive after rescaling}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Let each pairing value #{\beta_j} be a positive real number,
and let each double-coset size #{s_j} be positive. Suppose the
[class-function kernel](fdip-012C) takes real values and is
unchanged by inversion, and suppose each sum of its values in
the indicator matrix of \ref{fdip-013H} is positive. In an
ordered field containing the positive square root of each
size, divide the indicator of the #{j}-th coset by the
positive square root of #{s_j}. In that basis the rescaled
matrix is symmetric, and every entry is positive.}

\proof{
\p{\ref{fdip-012F} supplies the pairing as a
[double-coset kernel](fdip-012C). \ref{fdip-013H} writes the
indicator entry from the #{j}-th coset to the #{m}-th as the
product of the two pairing values with the sum of the declared
kernel values. The image of the #{j}-th indicator is constant
on the #{m}-th coset, and that constant is the indicator
entry, so the counting product of the #{m}-th indicator with
the image equals the indicator entry times #{s_m}.
\ref{fdip-012T} gives self-adjointness for the counting
product. The counting product of an indicator with itself is
the size of its coset, and \ref{fdip-012D} makes distinct
indicators disjoint, so the rescaled indicators are
orthonormal. The rescaled entry equals the counting product of
the #{m}-th indicator with the image of the #{j}-th, divided
by the positive square root of #{s_m s_j}. Each pairing value
is positive, each size is positive, and each kernel sum is
positive, so each entry is positive. Symmetry of the counting
product makes the rescaled matrix symmetric.}
}
